INT. OIL RIG - MEDICAL BAY - NIGHT

A steel-walled room bolted to the deck of a North Sea platform. A single examination cot, an oxygen tank strapped to the wall, a shelf of rattling supplies. The floor shudders with each wave. Rain hammers the window.

DR. ILSABET HALVORSEN-REYES, 40s, in rain-stained scrubs, bends over the cot. A headlamp hangs around her neck. Her eyes are red but steady.

On the cot lies WALLACE "WALLY" OKONJO, 50s, a roughneck, his shirt cut open. A pad of gauze soaks red above his ribs. He grits his teeth.

A radio on the wall crackles. The signal is thin.

CAPTAIN LOWE (V.O.)
(over radio)
Doctor, we're losing the chopper window. Fifteen minutes.

DR. HALVORSEN-REYES
(to the radio)
Understood.

WALLY
Doc...

DR. HALVORSEN-REYES
Don't talk.

WALLY
Is it bad?

DR. HALVORSEN-REYES
It's a rib and a lung. I've seen worse.

WALLY
Where?

DR. HALVORSEN-REYES
(to herself)
Not here.

She lifts the gauze. Looks. Presses the dressing back down with the heel of her hand. Wally hisses.

CAPTAIN LOWE (V.O.)
(over radio)
Everyone's standing by on deck. We can wait if you need more time.

DR. HALVORSEN-REYES
(to the radio, then to the empty room)
Nobody waits. Hear me? Nobody waits for me.

She opens a drawer. Pulls out a chest tube kit and tears the sterile wrapper with her teeth.

WALLY
Doc. If I don't...

DR. HALVORSEN-REYES
Wally.

WALLY
Tell my wife I kept the lighthouse key.

DR. HALVORSEN-REYES
You tell her yourself.

She slides the tube in. Wally shouts. Then gasps. Then, slowly, his chest rises, and his face loosens.

DR. HALVORSEN-REYES (CONT'D)
(to Wally)
There it is. Breathe with me.

He breathes. She breathes. A wave shakes the room. The window goes white with spray.

CAPTAIN LOWE (V.O.)
(over radio)
Doctor?

DR. HALVORSEN-REYES
(to the radio, calm)
Captain, you can send the chopper. He's stable.

CAPTAIN LOWE (V.O.)
(over radio)
Copy that. Good work, Doctor.

She switches off the radio. Slumps onto the stool. Wally lifts a hand. She takes it.
